stdout_text:
Looking at the samples, I find several groundedness problems: sample 3 ("Excellent performance on all fronts") gets avg_sub_score 2.8 and weakest_dimension "Seat Comfort" despite the text praising all aspects; sample 2 (all about seat/padding discomfort) picks weakest_dimension "Value For Money" though the text is about seat comfort. These weakest_dimension values appear driven by unseen numeric sub-scores rather than text, and can contradict the source text.

{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "avg_sub_score",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "rationale": "A composite average of the five rating dimensions is a reasonable summarizing axis, but it is largely redundant with Overall Rating and the individual dimension columns. Groundedness is mixed: for the glowing 'Excellent performance on all fronts' review it yields only 2.8, and for a 'dreadful seats' review it yields 3.4, suggesting it derives from underlying numeric sub-scores that at times conflict with the sentiment expressed, though sub-scores may be legitimately present."
    },
    {
      "name": "rating_gap",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "rationale": "The gap between overall rating and averaged sub-scores is a genuinely useful diagnostic axis for finding rows where overall dissatisfaction is not explained by the dimension scores — directly relevant to the not-recommended analysis. However several sample values look inconsistent (a rave review getting rating_gap 4.95, a dreadful-seat review getting -3.4), indicating the computation may not track the source reliably."
    },
    {
      "name": "weakest_dimension",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.35,
      "rationale": "Identifying the weakest rated dimension per review is highly useful for targeting improvements. But groundedness is weak: the seat-discomfort review is labelled 'Value For Money' and multiple all-praise reviews default to 'Seat Comfort', contradicting the narrative text. The label appears driven by tie-breaking or hidden numbers rather than a sound reading of the source."
    },
    {
      "name": "sub_score_variance",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "rationale": "Dispersion across dimensions can flag mixed experiences, a modest new axis. Yet sample values are questionable: two uniformly positive reviews get variance 0.0 while another equally positive one gets 4.2, and a review focused only on seats gets 2.3 — the values do not cohere with the text and cannot be verified from it."
    },
    {
      "name": "passenger_segment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "rationale": "An NPS-style promoter/passive/detractor bucket is a clean, directly useful grouping for the recommendation-focused query and is not a trivial restatement of Recommended. Values largely align with review sentiment (positive reviews = promoter, dreadful-seat review = detractor), so it is well grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "improvement_priority",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "rationale": "A categorical improvement lever (catering, service_quality, hardware_comfort, value_perception, ife) is exactly the actionable axis the query needs. However the seat-discomfort review is assigned 'value_perception' rather than hardware_comfort, showing the categorization can misread the source; the large NULL/none share for positive rows is appropriate behaviour and not penalised."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.53,
  "rationale": "The added columns provide several genuinely useful new axes for the improvement/recommendation analysis — an NPS segment, a weakest-dimension flag, a rating-vs-subscore gap, and an actionable improvement_priority category — well beyond trivial restatements. The main weakness is groundedness: multiple sample rows show weakest_dimension, rating_gap, sub_score_variance, and improvement_priority values that contradict or fail to track the source text (e.g. an all-praise review flagged with a weak Seat Comfort dimension and high variance, a seat-focused complaint labelled Value For Money). passenger_segment is the most reliable. Overall the set is analytically valuable but its derived numeric/categorical values are inconsistently supported by the underlying evidence."
}

